  "content": {
    "text": "Key Takeaways\n-\nElon Musk recently became the first person whose net worth surpassed $1 trillion, after his SpaceX's blockbuster IPO.\n-\nMost of Musk's estimated wealth is tied to his stock holdings in SpaceX and Tesla.\nThe world's richest person keeps getting richer.\nElon Musk became the world's first trillionaire Friday thanks to the blockbuster debut of his SpaceX (SPCX). His estimated net worth rose some $35.9 billion, to $1.3 trillion, Tuesday, according to a Forbes estimate, after SpaceX shares rose 5% in today's trading. (With that and Monday's 20% gain, they've surged nearly 50% from the company's IPO price.)\nMusk has held the title of world's richest on and off since 2021, though his recent gains have substantially widened his lead over other tech moguls. His net worth is now equivalent to that of the world's five next-richest people—Alphabet co-founders (GOOGL) Larry Page and Sergey Brin, Amazon (AMZN) founder Jeff Bezos, Oracle's (ORCL) Larry Ellison, and Dell Technologies (DELL) founder Michael Dell—combined, according to Forbes. Put another way, if Musk's wealth were a country's gross domestic product, he would rank ahead of all but 19 of the world's largest economies, according to International Monetary Fund data.\nMusk made his early fortune with the sale of his first significant venture, Zip2, in the late 1990s. He later co-founded X.com, which merged with Confinity to become PayPal (PYPL) and was acquired by eBay (EBAY) in the early 2000s. These days, most of Musk's wealth is through his stock holdings in SpaceX and electric vehicle maker Tesla, both of which he runs. He also has stakes in two other businesses he founded: brain interface company Neuralink and tunneling startup The Boring Company.\nHere's how the CEO's net worth surpassed $1 trillion—and why it keeps growing.\nSpaceX\nMusk owns 4.76 billion shares of SpaceX, according to company filings. Worth about $960 billion as of Tuesday's close, the SpaceX stake is by far the largest source of his wealth. The rocket maker and Starlink operator, which also encompasses X and xAI, reached a market value of about $2.6 trillion to finish Tuesday—after just three trading sessions.\nTesla\nMusk's second-greatest source of wealth is the CEO's roughly 11% stake in Tesla, worth about $1.52 billion as of Tuesday's close. Tesla has seen its stock slide nearly 20% from its December highs as it undergoes a transformation to focus more on physical applications of AI, such as autonomous vehicles and humanoid robots. Its market capitalization, which finished Tuesday's session around $1.52 trillion, made it the 11th most valuable public company in the world. Some market watchers have speculated that Musk might at some point merge Tesla and SpaceX.",
